      "perspective": "speaker",
      "analysis": "The speaker presents love as an enduring but variable emotional state, explicitly claiming that it is stronger now than it was previously."
    },
    {
      "perspective": "hearer",
      "analysis": "The direct address to “you” allows the avowal to function as interpersonal reassurance, but the passage supplies no response showing how the hearer construes it."
    },
    {
      "perspective": "narrator_or_text",
      "analysis": "The comparative construction frames the utterance primarily as a proposition about the speaker’s feelings across two periods, with the hearer’s changed dependence offered as the reason."
    },
    {
      "perspective": "reader",
      "analysis": "A reader can naturally take the statement as sincere emotional disclosure and, secondarily, as an enactment of closeness through the act of telling the hearer."
    }
  ],
  "features": [
    "quoted",
    "mixed_reading"
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "text": "I love you better now",
      "supports": "The present-tense avowal and comparative “better” directly report the speaker’s current loving state and its increased intensity."
    },
    {
      "text": "than I did in your state of proud independence",
      "supports": "The comparison with an earlier period treats love as a continuing mental or emotional state that can vary over time, strongly supporting a truth-conditional reading."
    },
    {
      "text": "when I can really be useful to you",
      "supports": "The speaker explains why the love is stronger now by reference to a changed practical relationship. This adds relational force but does not explicitly undertake a future commitment."
    },
    {
      "text": "you disdained every part but that of the giver and protector",
      "supports": "The contrast identifies a prior relational arrangement and helps make the avowal a reasoned assertion rather than an unstructured emotional exclamation."
    }
  ],
  "needs_more_context": true,
  "context_request": "The surrounding exchange, especially the hearer’s preceding claim and subsequent response, would clarify whether the utterance is also intended or received as reassurance, commitment, persuasion, or correction. Additional context could also bear on sincerity, though nothing here supports deception.",
  "typology_adequate": true,
  "typology_diagnosis": "The T/P/E typology gives a natural account. The occurrence is predominantly truth-conditional: it asserts and compares a loving state. It has limited performative force insofar as a direct avowal can enact intimacy or reassurance, but there is no explicit promise or undertaking. Its grammatical elaboration and causal comparison provide virtually no support for an exclamatory/reflexive reading.",
